A||||2||merlin||Do I need a new crankshaft?!||||8||
Z||000000||merlin||06-03-2001||07:58 PM||||I own at 1987 GT V6 and I’m in the process of changing the timing chain.  However, as I was pulling the crankshaft gear the puller stripped the threads in the nose of the crankshaft (the one that holds the harmonic balancer to the crank). How can I repair these threads?  There’s not much room for a drill, do I need to remove the crank?  Should I attempt to retap or use the Helicoil method?  Shame on me for not being more careful...||||reg||8
Z||000001||PBJ||06-03-2001||09:02 PM||||That is a good question. I can see how you could easily damage these threads with a puller. The bolt only holds the harmonic balancer from sliding off crank, there is a keyway that does all the work.  I would call an engine shop for sure fix but I can't seeing it to be damaged beyond chasing the threads with some kind of tap.||||reg||1
Z||000002||EdsB52||06-03-2001||10:00 PM||||I did sort of the same thing to my 454 truck crank. Actually I just changed the timing chain and was reassembling the mess as I tried to pull the harmonic balancer on with those threads. Well, GM finds it neccessary to have a .004 interference between the crank and H.B., so I ended up only grabbing a few threads and pulled 'em out. I ended up pulling the crank, having it drilled and helicoiled, and reinstalling the assy.  If the engines in the car, it will be very difficult to clean the threads. But if you have/pull the engine, you could probably just the entire assy to the machine shop.||||reg||1
